{
  "id": "binary-tree-vertical-order-traversal",
  "questionId": "binary-tree-vertical-order-traversal",
  "slug": "binary-tree-vertical-order-traversal",
  "title": "Binary Tree Vertical Order Traversal",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "BFS already visits nodes level by level and left to right within a level - if you also track each node's column as you enqueue it, does that give you the tie-breaking order for free?",
    "push (index, column) pairs onto the queue, starting with (root index, 0); when enqueuing a left child use column-1, and column+1 for a right child",
    "as you pop nodes off the queue (which happens in level order, left to right), append each value to a map keyed by column - the map's per-key lists come out already correctly ordered",
    "once BFS finishes, sort the map's keys (columns) numerically and read off the lists in that order to build the final result"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a DFS that visits left before right but forgetting to also sort by depth afterward, so same-column nodes from different levels can end up in the wrong relative order",
    "computing column with the parent's column plus/minus 1 incorrectly (mixing up which child gets -1 vs +1)",
    "forgetting to sort the final column keys numerically, leaving negative columns in the wrong position relative to positive ones"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a complete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`, which means \"no node here\"\n- every node is also assigned a column number: the root gets column 0, a left child gets its parent's column minus 1, and a right child gets its parent's column plus 1\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2 has no left child but does have a right child, 4.\n\n**Group the node values by column, ordered from the leftmost column to the rightmost column, and return the columns as a list of lists.**\n\nWithin a column, list values top to bottom by level; if two nodes land in the same column on the same level, list them in the order they're encountered scanning the tree left to right.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= length of tree <= 2000",
      "every real node value is in the range -100 to 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "the result has one entry per distinct column, ordered from leftmost to rightmost column"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree, which has exactly one column",
      "a tree that is a straight diagonal chain (all left children, or all right children), giving every node its own column",
      "multiple nodes sharing the same column at the same depth, requiring left-to-right tie-breaking"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "[[9], [3, 15], [20], [7]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[9], [3, 15], [20], [7]]",
        "explanation": "9 is column -1; 3 (root) and 15 are column 0 (15 is 20's left child, so column 1-1=0); 20 is column 1; 7 is column 2"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "[[4], [2], [1, 5, 6], [3], [7]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[4], [2], [1, 5, 6], [3], [7]]",
        "explanation": "columns range from -2 (node 4) to 2 (node 7); 1, 5, and 6 all land in column 0, and since 1 is on an earlier level it's listed first, then 5 and 6 in left-to-right encounter order"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, -101, 4]",
        "output": "[[2], [1, 4], [3]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[2], [1, 4], [3]]",
        "explanation": "2 is column -1; 1 (root) and 4 are column 0 (4 is 2's right child, so column -1+1=0); 3 is column 1"
      }
    ],
